# Talyvor Docs — environment template.

DOCS_DATABASE_URL=postgres://docs:changeme@localhost:5432/talyvor_docs?sslmode=disable
DOCS_LISTEN_ADDR=0.0.0.0:4000

# debug | info | warn | error. Now actually honoured — it was parsed into Config and then
# never applied to the slog handler, so this was accepted and silently inert.
DOCS_LOG_LEVEL=info

# WebSocket Origin allow-list (comma-separated). EMPTY = SAME-ORIGIN ONLY, which is what you
# want behind a reverse proxy serving the SPA and API from one hostname.
#
# Set this ONLY for a split-origin setup — e.g. the dev frontend on :5174 talking to the API
# on :4000. Previously collab accepted EVERY origin unconditionally, under a comment claiming
# an env var tightened it in production; no such variable existed. This is that variable.
#   DOCS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:5174
DOCS_ALLOWED_ORIGINS=

# SEC-4 Layer 1 root of trust: the shared secret the edge gateway sends in the
# x-gateway-auth header to prove a request transited it — which is what makes the
# injected x-user-email trustworthy. Deliberately UNPREFIXED (not DOCS_*): it is the
# same secret the gateway signs for Track.
#
# REQUIRED, and INTENTIONALLY BLANK below. This template previously shipped a working
# 42-character placeholder, which satisfied the >= 16 boot check — so copying .env.example
# to .env produced a bootable deployment whose root of trust was published in this repo.
# Anyone with the value can forge x-gateway-auth + x-user-email and act as any user in any
# workspace. That placeholder is in git history, cannot be un-published, and is now
# permanently rejected at boot (see publishedGatewaySecrets in internal/config).
#
# Generate your own, and use the same value the gateway signs:
#   openssl rand -hex 32
GATEWAY_AUTH_SECRET=

# Single-tenant default. Phase 5 will iterate workspaces from the DB.
DOCS_DEFAULT_WORKSPACE=default

# Compose-only — sets the Postgres password used by the postgres service and referenced in
# DOCS_DATABASE_URL above. REQUIRED, and blank here for the same reason as the gateway secret:
# compose declares it `${POSTGRES_PASSWORD:?…}`, so `docker compose up` refuses rather than
# silently starting with a committed credential. It previously defaulted to `changeme`.
#   openssl rand -hex 16
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# Track integration (optional). When set, /v1/embed pulls live data,
# the right panel shows backlinks, and a 15-min cost syncer rolls up
# Track issue AI spend into pages.ai_cost_usd.
#
# DOCS_TRACK_API_KEY is a SERVICE credential: Docs presents it to Track on behalf of the
# instance, not the end user. The workspace in the request path is therefore authorized
# against the caller's verified memberships BEFORE the call is made — without that check the
# key can be pointed at any tenant (fixed in internal/trackintegration).
DOCS_TRACK_URL=
DOCS_TRACK_API_KEY=

# Member-sync pull from Track's GET /v1/service/members. A DEDICATED secret, separate from
# DOCS_TRACK_API_KEY — never reuse them. Unset ⇒ member sync is disabled. Was read by the
# code but missing from this template entirely.
DOCS_TRACK_MEMBER_SYNC_SECRET=

# Lens integration. When set, the AI slash commands + Ask AI panel
# proxy through Lens. AI features short-circuit to an "unavailable"
# state when these are empty.
DOCS_LENS_URL=http://localhost:8080
DOCS_LENS_API_KEY=

# DOCS_SHARE_SECRET and DOCS_BASE_URL were listed here but are read NOWHERE in the code,
# and both descriptions were false:
#
#   DOCS_SHARE_SECRET claimed to sign share-link tokens. Nothing signs them — a token is 32
#   bytes of crypto/rand (internal/sharing/store.go newToken), which needs no secret and is
#   the right design. Setting the variable did nothing, while implying a signing scheme
#   existed to audit.
#
#   DOCS_BASE_URL claimed to be composed into user-facing URLs. Share URLs are emitted
#   host-relative ("/s/{token}") precisely because the server does not know its public
#   hostname; the SPA resolves them against its own origin. Setting it changed nothing.
#
# Removed rather than implemented: a template that lists variables with no effect teaches
# operators to distrust the whole file.
